In v7, `onChunk` now receives **all** stream part types (including lifecycle, boundary, and terminal events like `start`, `finish`, `error`, etc.), whereas in v6 it only received a subset like text deltas, reasoning deltas, and tool calls [1][2]. Update your handler to guard against the specific chunk types you process:

```ts
streamText({
  model: yourModel,
  prompt: 'Hello',
  onChunk({ chunk }) {
    if (chunk.type === 'text-delta') {
      console.log(chunk.text);
    }
    // Add other type checks as needed
  },
});
```

In v7, the top-level `response` property is deprecated. Use `finalStep.response.body` instead:

```ts
import { generateText } from 'ai';

const result = await generateText({
  model: 'anthropic/claude-sonnet-5.5',
  prompt: 'Hello, world!',
});

console.log(result.finalStep.response.body); // [1][4]
```

If you were using the deprecated `response.body` directly, it will be empty because that property is no longer populated in v7. The correct path is always through `finalStep.response` for both headers and body. [7]

In Vercel AI SDK v7, the default behavior for HTTP redirects in MCP transports changed from `'follow'` to `'error'` to prevent server-side request forgery (SSRF) attacks [1][3]. If your MCP HTTP server sits behind a redirect, you need to explicitly allow redirects in your transport configuration:

```typescript
import { createMCPClient } from '@ai-sdk/mcp';

const mcpClient = await createMCPClient({
  transport: {
    type: 'http',
    url: 'https://your-server.com/mcp',
    redirect: 'follow', // Add this line to allow redirects
  },
});
```

This change was introduced in v7 to improve security by rejecting redirects by default [1][3].

In AI SDK 7, `xai(modelId)` uses the **Responses API** by default [1].

To keep using the **Chat Completions API**, use `xai.chat(modelId)` instead [1].

